      "updated": 1791634248359,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "                                            VECTOR CLOCKS/CONFLICT RESOLUTION\n\nThe Problem: What Does “Before” Mean in a Distributed System?\n\nOn a single machine, time is a total order. Event A at timestamp 10:00:00.100 definitely happened before Event B at 10:00:00.200.\n\nIn a distributed system, clocks drift. Machine A might be 50ms ahead of Machine B. An event “at 10:00:00.100” on Machine A might actually \nhave occurred AFTER an event “at 10:00:00.200” on Machine B (because Machine B’s clock is fast).\n\nYou cannot use wall-clock timestamps to determine causality across machines. You need a different mechanism.\n\nLamport Clocks: The simplest solution. Each node maintains a logical counter. On any event, increment the counter. On send, include the \ncounter. On receive, set local counter to max(local, received) + 1.\n\nLamport clocks can tell you if A happened before B. But if neither happened before the other (they’re concurrent), Lamport clocks can’t tell \nyou that — you just don’t know if they’re concurrent or if one caused the other.\n\nVector Clocks: The full solution. They track causality across ALL nodes.\n\n                                                                    HOW VECTOR CLOCK WORKS\n\nEach node maintains a vector (array) of counters — one counter per node in the system.\n\nFor 3 nodes (A, B, C), each vector has 3 positions:\n\n[A's events, B's events, C's events]\nNode A's initial vector: [0, 0, 0]\nNode B's initial vector: [0, 0, 0]\nNode C's initial vector: [0, 0, 0]\n\nRules:\n\n- On a local event: increment your own position in the vector\n- On a send: increment your own position, attach your vector to the message\n- On a receive: take element-wise maximum of your vector and received vector, then increment your own position\n\nWALK THROUGH\n\nInitial state:\nNode A: [0, 0, 0]\nNode B: [0, 0, 0]\nNode C: [0, 0, 0]\n\nEvent 1: Node A does a local event\nNode A: [1, 0, 0]\n\nEvent 2: Node A sends a message to Node B\nNode A: [2, 0, 0]  (increments before sending)\nNode B receives [2, 0, 0]:\n  Node B's vector before: [0, 0, 0]\n  Element-wise max: [max(0,2), max(0,0), max(0,0)] = [2, 0, 0]\n  Increment B's position: [2, 1, 0]\nNode B: [2, 1, 0]\n\nEvent 3: Node C does a local event (independently, no communication)\nNode C: [0, 0, 1]\n\nEvent 4: Node B sends a message to Node C\nNode B: [2, 2, 0]  (increments before sending)\nNode C receives [2, 2, 0]:\n  Node C's vector before: [0, 0, 1]\n  Element-wise max: [max(0,2), max(0,2), max(1,0)] = [2, 2, 1]\n  Increment C's position: [2, 2, 2]\nNode C: [2, 2, 2]\n\n\n                                                          DETERMINING CAUSALITY WITH VECTOR CLOCKS\n\nGiven two vector clocks V1 and V2:\n\nV1 happened before V2 if: every element of V1 ≤ corresponding element of V2, AND at least one element of V1 < V2.\n\nV1 and V2 are concurrent if: neither V1 ≤ V2 nor V2 ≤ V1. Some elements of V1 are greater, some elements of V2 are greater.\n\nV1 = [2, 1, 0]\nV2 = [2, 2, 2]\n\nCompare element by element:\nA: 2 ≤ 2 ✅\nB: 1 ≤ 2 ✅\nC: 0 ≤ 2 ✅\n\nAll elements of V1 ≤ V2, and some are strictly less (B and C)\n→ V1 happened before V2 ✅\n\n\nV1 = [3, 1, 0]\nV2 = [2, 2, 0]\n\nCompare:\nA: 3 > 2 ← V1 is greater here\nB: 1 < 2 ← V2 is greater here\nC: 0 = 0\n\nNeither is ≤ the other.\n→ V1 and V2 are CONCURRENT (neither caused the other)\n\n\nCONFLICT RESOLUTION — WHAT TO DO WITH CURRENT EVENTS\n\nWhen two events are concurrent, they might represent conflicting updates to the same piece of data. Different systems resolve this differently:\n\nLast Write Wins (LWW)\n\nUse a timestamp to pick a winner. The most recent write (by wall clock or Lamport timestamp) wins.\n\njavascript:\n\nconst resolve = (version1, version2) => {\n    return version1.timestamp > version2.timestamp ? version1 : version2;\n};\n\nProblem: Timestamps can be wrong (clock skew). You might silently discard a valid write. Amazon learned this the hard way with shopping cart \ndata.\n\nWhen to use: Low-stakes data where losing an update is acceptable. Session data, cache entries, last-seen-at timestamps.\n\n                                                                        MERGE VALUES (CRDTs)\n\nConflict-Free Replicated Data Types are designed so concurrent updates can always be merged without data loss. The merge operation is commu-\ntative, associative, and idempotent.\n\nEXAMPLES:\n\nG-Counter (Grow-only Counter):\n\nEach node has its own counter that only increments.\nNode A's view: [A:5, B:3, C:2] → total: 10\nNode B's view: [A:5, B:4, C:2] → total: 11 (B incremented locally)\n\nMerge: take max of each → [A:5, B:4, C:2] → total: 11\n\nNo conflict possible — you take the max of each element. Works for: page view counts, like counts, download counts.\n\nOR-Set (Observed-Remove Set):\n\nAdd \"apple\": generate unique tag, add (apple, tag1)\nConcurrent add \"apple\" on another node: (apple, tag2)\nRemove \"apple\" on first node: remove all tags seen at time of removal → removes tag1\nMerge: (apple, tag2) still exists! Apple is in the set (because only tag1 was removed)\n\nThis handles the “add and remove concurrently” case correctly.\n\nUsed by: Redis CRDT (Enterprise), Riak, distributed caching systems, collaborative text editors (operational transforms or CRDTs power Goo-\ngle Docs).\n\nApplication-Level Resolution\n\nSend both versions to the application. Let the application (or user) decide.\n\njavascript:\n\nconst resolve = (version1, version2) => {\n    return {\n        conflict: true,\n        versions: [version1, version2],\n        resolution: null // to be filled by application\n    };\n};\n\nAmazon Dynamo sends all conflicting shopping cart versions to the UI. The application (or user) resolves by merging them — usually by taking \nthe union of all items in all conflicting cart versions.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                                            VECTOR CLOCKS/CONFLICT RESOLUTION\n\nThe Problem: What Does “Before” Mean in a Distributed System?\n\nOn a single machine, time is a total order. Event A at timestamp 10:00:00.100 definitely happened before Event B at 10:00:00.200.\n\nIn a distributed system, clocks drift. Machine A might be 50ms ahead of Machine B. An event “at 10:00:00.100” on Machine A might actually \nhave occurred AFTER an event “at 10:00:00.200” on Machine B (because Machine B’s clock is fast).\n\nYou cannot use wall-clock timestamps to determine causality across machines. You need a different mechanism.\n\nLamport Clocks: The simplest solution. Each node maintains a logical counter. On any event, increment the counter. On send, include the \ncounter. On receive, set local counter to max(local, received) + 1.\n\nLamport clocks can tell you if A happened before B. But if neither happened before the other (they’re concurrent), Lamport clocks can’t tell \nyou that — you just don’t know if they’re concurrent or if one caused the other.\n\nVector Clocks: The full solution. They track causality across ALL nodes.\n\n                                                                    HOW VECTOR CLOCK WORKS\n\nEach node maintains a vector (array) of counters — one counter per node in the system.\n\nFor 3 nodes (A, B, C), each vector has 3 positions:\n\n[A's events, B's events, C's events]\nNode A's initial vector: [0, 0, 0]\nNode B's initial vector: [0, 0, 0]\nNode C's initial vector: [0, 0, 0]\n\nRules:\n\n- On a local event: increment your own position in the vector\n- On a send: increment your own position, attach your vector to the message\n- On a receive: take element-wise maximum of your vector and received vector, then increment your own position\n\nWALK THROUGH\n\nInitial state:\nNode A: [0, 0, 0]\nNode B: [0, 0, 0]\nNode C: [0, 0, 0]\n\nEvent 1: Node A does a local event\nNode A: [1, 0, 0]\n\nEvent 2: Node A sends a message to Node B\nNode A: [2, 0, 0]  (increments before sending)\nNode B receives [2, 0, 0]:\n  Node B's vector before: [0, 0, 0]\n  Element-wise max: [max(0,2), max(0,0), max(0,0)] = [2, 0, 0]\n  Increment B's position: [2, 1, 0]\nNode B: [2, 1, 0]\n\nEvent 3: Node C does a local event (independently, no communication)\nNode C: [0, 0, 1]\n\nEvent 4: Node B sends a message to Node C\nNode B: [2, 2, 0]  (increments before sending)\nNode C receives [2, 2, 0]:\n  Node C's vector before: [0, 0, 1]\n  Element-wise max: [max(0,2), max(0,2), max(1,0)] = [2, 2, 1]\n  Increment C's position: [2, 2, 2]\nNode C: [2, 2, 2]\n\n\n                                                          DETERMINING CAUSALITY WITH VECTOR CLOCKS\n\nGiven two vector clocks V1 and V2:\n\nV1 happened before V2 if: every element of V1 ≤ corresponding element of V2, AND at least one element of V1 < V2.\n\nV1 and V2 are concurrent if: neither V1 ≤ V2 nor V2 ≤ V1. Some elements of V1 are greater, some elements of V2 are greater.\n\nV1 = [2, 1, 0]\nV2 = [2, 2, 2]\n\nCompare element by element:\nA: 2 ≤ 2 ✅\nB: 1 ≤ 2 ✅\nC: 0 ≤ 2 ✅\n\nAll elements of V1 ≤ V2, and some are strictly less (B and C)\n→ V1 happened before V2 ✅\n\n\nV1 = [3, 1, 0]\nV2 = [2, 2, 0]\n\nCompare:\nA: 3 > 2 ← V1 is greater here\nB: 1 < 2 ← V2 is greater here\nC: 0 = 0\n\nNeither is ≤ the other.\n→ V1 and V2 are CONCURRENT (neither caused the other)\n\n\nCONFLICT RESOLUTION — WHAT TO DO WITH CURRENT EVENTS\n\nWhen two events are concurrent, they might represent conflicting updates to the same piece of data. Different systems resolve this differently:\n\nLast Write Wins (LWW)\n\nUse a timestamp to pick a winner. The most recent write (by wall clock or Lamport timestamp) wins.\n\njavascript:\n\nconst resolve = (version1, version2) => {\n    return version1.timestamp > version2.timestamp ? version1 : version2;\n};\n\nProblem: Timestamps can be wrong (clock skew). You might silently discard a valid write. Amazon learned this the hard way with shopping cart \ndata.\n\nWhen to use: Low-stakes data where losing an update is acceptable. Session data, cache entries, last-seen-at timestamps.\n\n                                                                        MERGE VALUES (CRDTs)\n\nConflict-Free Replicated Data Types are designed so concurrent updates can always be merged without data loss. The merge operation is commu-\ntative, associative, and idempotent.\n\nEXAMPLES:\n\nG-Counter (Grow-only Counter):\n\nEach node has its own counter that only increments.\nNode A's view: [A:5, B:3, C:2] → total: 10\nNode B's view: [A:5, B:4, C:2] → total: 11 (B incremented locally)\n\nMerge: take max of each → [A:5, B:4, C:2] → total: 11\n\nNo conflict possible — you take the max of each element. Works for: page view counts, like counts, download counts.\n\nOR-Set (Observed-Remove Set):\n\nAdd \"apple\": generate unique tag, add (apple, tag1)\nConcurrent add \"apple\" on another node: (apple, tag2)\nRemove \"apple\" on first node: remove all tags seen at time of removal → removes tag1\nMerge: (apple, tag2) still exists! Apple is in the set (because only tag1 was removed)\n\nThis handles the “add and remove concurrently” case correctly.\n\nUsed by: Redis CRDT (Enterprise), Riak, distributed caching systems, collaborative text editors (operational transforms or CRDTs power Goo-\ngle Docs).\n\nApplication-Level Resolution\n\nSend both versions to the application. Let the application (or user) decide.\n\njavascript:\n\nconst resolve = (version1, version2) => {\n    return {\n        conflict: true,\n        versions: [version1, version2],\n        resolution: null // to be filled by application\n    };\n};\n\nAmazon Dynamo sends all conflicting shopping cart versions to the UI. The application (or user) resolves by merging them — usually by taking \nthe union of all items in all conflicting cart versions.",
